Kally Duncan (Sonia), right, and Sherry Hopkins (Ella) in Life Sucks at Stage West in Fort Worth.(Tom Fox / Staff Photographer) By Nancy Churnin 12:00 PM on Oct 25, 2017 CDT FORT WORTH — A dazzling Application Pending, a one-woman show, featuring Janelle Lutz playing 43 characters at Circle Theatre. A deliciously subversive and immersive Life Sucks, a contemporary take on Uncle Vanya at Stage West. And a disturbing and provocative take on Leonardo da Vinci in A Lost Leonardo at Amphibian Stage Productions, serendipitously timed to the release of Walter Isaacson's new biography on the Renaissance artist. Janelle Lutz stars as Christine Evans in Application Pending by Greg Edwards and Andy Sandberg, presented by Circle Theatre in Fort Worth through Nov. 18. (Tim Long) You'll find these shows in cozy, intimate theaters no more than five miles apart in Fort Worth. Recently, some of Dallas' hottest actors have been taking advantage of the opportunities to dig into regional premieres of work by contemporary playwrights. Multiple Dallas actors have made their Fort Worth debuts this season. And Stage West in Fort Worth now offers a voucher to a play at Kitchen Dog Theater in Dallas as part of its season subscription; Kitchen is offering a voucher to a Stage West show in its season package, too. Kally Duncan (Sonia), right, and Sherry Hopkins (Ella) in Life Sucks at Stage West in Fort Worth(Tom Fox / Staff Photographer) For Tim Long, managing director at Circle Theatre, it adds up to a deeper bench of talent for patrons and more opportunities for Dallas-Fort Worth actors, which brings the region a step closer to becoming a hub that can support working actors. "Everybody benefits when actors have multiple places to showcase their work," Long says. Lutz, a Lyric Stage and Brick Road Theatre favorite, is making her debut at Circle Theatre with a tour de force performance in a regional premiere by Greg Edwards and Andy Sandberg that takes place in the pre-primary admissions office at a prestigious private school. Janelle Lutz stars as Christine Evans in Application Pending by Greg Edwards and Andy Sandberg, presented by
Circle Theatre in Fort Worth through Nov. 18.(Tim Long) Lutz, justly appreciated for her knockout singing voice, proves herself a deft comedienne and dramatic actress as Christine, a kindergarten assistant who has been thrust into the position of admissions officer when the previous person in the office leaves "to pursue other opportunities," the headmaster instructs her to say — a euphemism that sets up multiple punch lines. The increasingly frazzled Christine fields calls from angry and anxious parents, a conniving admissions officer from a rival school and more. Lutz, masterfully directed by Jennifer Engler, weaves an emotional journey through the spot-on characterizations. We see her move from overwhelmed to determined and ultimately changed for the better in a story that spans the length of her school day. Stage West scored a hit with a previous Aaron Posner twist on a Chekhov classic, Stupid [Expletive] Bird, a contemporary riff on Chekhov's The Seagull in January. It deserves another one with the witty and touching regional premiere of Life Sucks, Posner's contemporary spin on Uncle Vanya. Under the deft direction of Emily Scott Banks, a terrific cast breaks down the fourth wall, moving their attention from audience to play and back, bringing home the funny mixed with the sad as characters yearn for who and what they can't have. The patron sitting next to me on opening night didn't know Uncle Vanya and was swept up in Kally Duncan's vulnerable young Sonia, Cindee Mayfield's brittle, wise Babs and all the hapless men, from Jakie Cabe's dashing Dr. Aster to Mark Shum's flailing Vanya to Michael Corolla's officious Professor, who drive Sonia and Babs crazy by lusting after Sherry Hopkins' exasperated Ella. Stage West: Life Sucks at Stage West Theatre $17 – $35 View Event Meanwhile, Sherry Jo Ward, who does a scene-stealing turn as the eccentric Pickles, can also be seen on Tuesday nights through Oct. 31 at Stage West's smaller studio space, performing her one-woman show, Stiff. This autobiographical, must-see piece, a hit at this year's Festival of Independent Theatres in Dallas, is the wry, true-
life story of the actress' journey with Stiff Person Syndrome, a rare neurological disease. Just a few miles away at Amphibian Stage, David Davalos' tantalizing A Lost Leonardo offers a fascinating, speculative historical riff on the life of a genius with insatiable curiosity. Davalos, a University of Texas at Austin alumnus, scored a 2011 off-Broadway hit with the historically inspired Wittenberg. This audacious play, which benefited from development in Amphibian's staged reading series, offers us a Leonardo at a crossroads between his art and his fascination with creating military weapons. The cast, directed by New York-based Fort Worth native Illana Stein, features Matthew Amendt's intense Leonardo; Patrick Bynane's calculating Machiavelli; Jim Jorgensen's ingratiatingly evil Cesare Borgia, Kelsey Milbourn's yearning Lucrezia Borgia and Jenna Anderson's multiple characters, but particularly her spirited, mysterious Lisabella. So get thee to Fort Worth. You won't regret it. Plan your life APPLICATION PENDING continues through Nov. 18 at Circle Theatre, 230 W. 4th St., Fort Worth. 817-877-3040. $15-$38. circletheatre.com. Performance reviewed was Saturday matinee. Running time: 1 hour, 27 mins. LIFE SUCKS continues through Nov. 12 at Stage West, 821 W. Vickery Blvd., Fort Worth. $31-$35. stagewest.org. Performance reviewed was Oct. 14. Running time: 2 hours, 20 mins. A LOST LEONARDO continues through Nov. 5 at Amphibian Stage Productions, 120 S. Main St., Fort Worth. $33. amphibianstage.com. Performance reviewed was Oct. 15. Running time: 2 hours, 33 mins. STIFF continues through Oct. 31 at Stage West, 821 W. Vickery Blvd., Fort Worth. $20. stagewest.org. Performance reviewed was July 15 at Festival of Independent Theatres at Bath House Cultural Center, 521 E. Lawther Drive, Dallas. Running time: 50 mins. Stiff at Stage West Studio Theatre Starting at $20 View Event Circle Theatre: Application Pending at Circle Theatre $20 – $38 View Event Amphibian Stage Productio